 ['Largest jar diameter including label seam','Jar height including lid and any dust cover','Filled glass weight and center of gravity','Window position relative to the candle label','Film attachment and clearance from the glass','Base lock and vertical insert support','Ribbon route and bow clearance','Transport carton and glass protection'],
 [
 ('Make the candle visible without losing its identity', '''The burgundy Christmas candle window box puts a single glass jar behind a rectangular front opening. A peaked upper silhouette and gold-colored bow frame the candle rather than competing with a large printed product picture. This is a useful direction for home-fragrance brands whose jar color, wax appearance or label should remain visible on a seasonal shelf. The pictured jar is a styling reference; it does not establish the product supplied with an order. Start with the actual candle, including its lid and label, and decide which information should be readable through the opening. Place the brand name on the upper front area, away from the bow. Keep scent identity on a panel that remains visible when several cartons stand together.'''),
 ('Measure the glass before choosing carton proportions', '''Use the largest outside diameter of the finished jar, not a nominal candle capacity. Include a raised label seam, lid overhang, decorative band and any wrap that increases the diameter. Measure the overall closed height and identify where the jar carries its weight. A tightly fitted wall can scuff the label; an oversized cavity allows the glass to rotate until the label disappears behind the window edge. Set the insert cavity and wall clearance together. Ask for a blank sample with the real candle installed, then inspect the window alignment from normal shelf height. Record the accepted internal dimensions in the sample brief so a change of jar supplier does not silently change the packaging fit.'''),
 ('Treat the display opening as a structural decision', '''A front window removes material from a load-bearing panel. The remaining border, corner radii and distance to fold lines must be considered with board grade and packed weight. The image shows a transparent-looking opening, but cannot confirm whether there is film, an uncovered cutout or a simulated reflection. Specify the intended window construction explicitly. If film is selected, request its material, attachment area and position relative to the jar. Check for wrinkles, loose edges and adhesive visible from the front. If the opening is uncovered, assess dust exposure and access to the contents during retail handling. Neither option by itself proves that the carton protects glass in a delivery network.'''),
 ('Build support below the jar, not just around it', '''A paperboard cradle, collar or platform may help locate the candle, but the support must work with the base closure. A heavy glass jar should not rely on a decorative front border to remain upright. Discuss how its weight transfers through the insert to the bottom panel and how the bottom is locked or bonded. Test the filled carton while lifting it from its body, setting it down and removing the product. Check whether the candle tips into the window, rubs the film or slips beneath a collar. The visualization does not show an insert, so its shape and material are additional design decisions. Keep the protective components visible in the quotation rather than assuming they are included.'''),
 ('Reserve artwork space for scent and care information', '''The concept places small stars, evergreen sprigs and gold-colored ornament around a burgundy panel. These visual cues can support a holiday collection while leaving a practical label area. Distinguish the candle name from seasonal greetings and keep scent variants legible at actual print size. Put the barcode, batch reference and required candle-use information on a suitable solid panel, using the candle brand's approved wording. Avoid printing essential text across the cut edge or fold. If metallic foil is requested, define the foil area separately from the printed botanical art. A gold appearance in the supplied images is not evidence of foil stamping or a particular coating; confirm the finish on the selected paper.'''),
 ('Keep the ribbon separate from the lifting method', '''The bow sits at the peaked top, creating a gift-ready focal point. Its visible position does not establish how it is fixed or whether the ribbon wraps beneath the box. Specify ribbon width, color, route, attachment and the stage at which it is tied. If the bow is purely decorative, recipients should have a clear way to open the carton without pulling against the glass. Assess overhead clearance when the carton sits in a retail tray or transit case. A bow compressed against adjacent boxes can arrive with a different shape from the concept. Do not assume ribbon strength or a safe carry load from the picture. Validate any functional lifting arrangement with the filled sample.'''),
 ('Plan candle fulfillment and outer protection', '''Decide whether the candle carton is handed over in a shop, delivered in a corporate gift set or shipped individually to a customer. Each route changes the protective brief. For parcel delivery, assess an outer corrugated shipper and cushioning with the actual jar and carton; the display box is a presentation component until testing establishes otherwise. Record how many upright units go in each master carton and whether partitions prevent glass-to-glass contact. Check the condition of windows, corners, bows and labels after the handling agreed for the route. Include packing instructions for the fulfillment team, particularly if ribbon tying and insert assembly occur after candle filling. Freight calculations need the final packed dimensions, not just the decorative box size.'''),
 ('Compare the candle window with a closed presentation box', '''Choose this direction when the candle itself should be visible before purchase and a vertical shelf footprint suits the jar. A closed ribbon presentation box may be more useful for a candle plus matches or a multi-item gift set that needs a broader insert. A carry carton addresses handover, but adds handle-load requirements. Make the decision from the actual retail interaction: must the shopper see the jar label, must staff assemble sets quickly, and does the recipient need a protected glass product during delivery? Keep those requirements in the request for quotation. The important distinction is not simply the burgundy color; it is the relationship among window visibility, upright fit, bottom support and the selected distribution route.''')],
